Here, we present a transferable and accurate machine learning potential for cementitious systems, CSH-MLP, which is developed from a high-quality dataset of over 50,000 configurations derived nearly 700 distinct C-S-H systems, spanning diverse compositions, defect structures and hydration states. The CSH-MLP achieves near-DFT accuracy in predicting structural and mechanical properties and resolves long-standing discrepancies with high-pressure X-ray diffraction by capturing the interplay between defect chemistry, interlayer densification, and silicate chain connectivity. It demonstrates excellent transferability to large, amorphous, and nanoporous systems well beyond the training set. Offering more than an order-of-magnitude speedup over reactive force fields, this approach enables quantum-accurate, large-scale, long-timescale, and multi-phase simulations of cement systems.